  "ai_overview": {
    "text_blocks": [
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "Both ACE (angiotensin-converting enzyme) inhibitors and ARBs (angiotensin II receptor blockers) are equally recommended as class I first-line therapies for managing essential hypertension.",
        "snippet_highlighted_words": [
          "Both ACE (angiotensin-converting enzyme) inhibitors and ARBs (angiotensin II receptor blockers) are equally recommended as class I first-line therapies for managing essential hypertension"
        ],
        "reference_indexes": [
          0
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "Efficacy"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Blood Pressure Lowering: Both drug classes lower blood pressure effectively by blocking the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system (RAAS), with no significant difference in long-term cardiovascular event prevention or overall efficacy.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              0,
              1,
              2
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Guideline Standing: Major guidelines (such as the ACC/AHA and ESC/ESH) list ACE inhibitors, ARBs, calcium channel blockers (CCBs), and thiazide diuretics as equal first-line options.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              3
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "Key Differences & Safety Profiles"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Side Effects (Cough): ACE inhibitors frequently cause a dry, persistent cough due to a buildup of bradykinin. ARBs do not block bradykinin breakdown and rarely cause this cough.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              2,
              4,
              5
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Angioedema: Both can rarely cause angioedema (tissue swelling), though the risk is higher with ACE inhibitors.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              6
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Tolerability: Real-world studies and clinical data show that ARBs generally have a better safety profile and lower discontinuation rates than ACE inhibitors, leading some clinicians to preferentially prescribe ARBs.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              0
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Combination Warning: Never combine an ACE inhibitor and an ARB together, as dual blockade increases the risk of side effects like hyperkalemia (high potassium) and worsening kidney function without improving cardiovascular outcomes.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              6,
              7,
              8
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "You can learn more about how these classes compare via the American Heart Association Medication Guide .",
        "snippet_links": [
          {
            "text": "American Heart Association Medication Guide",
            "link": "https://www.heart.org/en/health-topics/heart-failure/treatment-options-for-heart-failure/medications-used-to-treat-heart-failure"
          }
        ],
        "reference_indexes": [
          7
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "To help tailor this information, let me know:"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Are you comparing these for yourself or someone else ?"
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Are there any other conditions present (such as diabetes, kidney disease, or a history of cough)?"
          }
        ]
      }
